From fada5d8553e9794f0404d6b54bc7da0154eeb25f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: woahwhattheheck <293286387+woahwhattheheck@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 04:10:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] obs-vst3: Add out-of-process VST3 audio filter

Adds a "VST3 Plug-in" audio filter that hosts VST3 effects through the
Steinberg VST3 SDK directly, as requested by the VST3 Support RFP
(discussion #5074).

Plug-ins never run inside OBS. Every filter instance owns an
obs-vst3-host process which loads the plug-in:

- Audio is exchanged through an anonymous shared memory region (planar
  float input, sidechain and output planes); a fixed-size request and
  reply on a dedicated socket pair (named pipe on Windows) hands each
  block of up to 1024 frames over. Commands (load, configure, state,
  editor) use a second channel with length-prefixed messages.
- If the bridge crashes, hangs (500 ms per block) or is restarting, the
  filter passes audio through unchanged. The host is restarted
  automatically with the last known plug-in state.
- Plug-in editors are top-level windows owned by the bridge process
  (X11 with the IRunLoop of the bridge on Linux, Win32 and Cocoa on
  Windows and macOS), so OBS itself never needs X11 or cross-process
  window embedding.

Modules are discovered in the standard VST3 locations (plus VST3_PATH
and the Flatpak plug-in extension on Linux). Each new or changed module
is inspected by a short-lived bridge process (moduleinfo.json first,
the factory otherwise), and results are cached in the module config
directory. Every audio effect class of a module is listed, which covers
"shell" plug-ins exposing several effects.

The plug-in's component and controller state is stored base64 encoded
in the filter settings and restored when the filter is created. Buses
are configured to match the OBS speaker layout; channels a plug-in does
not process are passed through. Plug-ins with an aux input bus can use
another source as sidechain through the same capture path as the
compressor filter. The reported latency is shown in the properties.
